    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass. The `verify` build fails only on the JaCoCo coverage gate (0.21 line coverage vs. 0.85 required) \u2014 but that is a pre-existing, repository-wide condition: the project ships with only 3 acceptance tests covering a large Spring app, so the gate can't be met by that suite regardless of my change. The test suite itself (`mvn test`) is fully green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional, city-region-validated `postcode` support for owners:\n\n**Validation (the core rule)**\n- `util/PostcodePolicy.java` \u2014 new. Holds the fixed region\u2192postcode-range table (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099) and decides validity for a city, reusing the existing `LocalityResolver` for city\u2192region lookup. Unknown region \u21d2 any 4-digit postcode accepted.\n- `rest/validation/ValidPostcodeForCity.java` + `...Validator.java` \u2014 new class-level Bean Validation constraint on the request DTO. It skips when postcode is absent or not 4 digits (format is the field pattern's job) and otherwise delegates the range check to `PostcodePolicy`, reporting the violation on the `postcode` field. Mirrors the existing `TelephoneDigits` validator pattern.\n\n**API contract**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added optional `postcode` to `OwnerFields` (`pattern: ^[0-9]{4}$` gives the malformed-\u2192400 for free) and stamped the class-level `@ValidPostcodeForCity` on the schema via `x-class-extra-annotation`. Because `Owner` composes `OwnerFields`, postcode is returned on GET automatically.\n\n**Persistence & wiring**\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added the `postcode` column + accessors.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.updateOwner` \u2014 added `setPostcode(...)` (the create path already maps it via MapStruct name-matching).\n- Added a `postcode` column to all four `schema.sql` dialects (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres).\n\nEach piece sits in its own small unit: format validation stays in the field constraint, the city\u2194postcode business rule lives in `PostcodePolicy`, and the resolver table is reused rather than duplicated. The three new acceptance tests (`coreStoresValidPostcode`, `coreAcceptsOwnerWithoutPostcode`, `errorRejectsMalformedPostcode`) all pass.",
